          as 109 discrete interrogatory subparts, in excess of the limit of 50 discrete subparts established by

          the New Mexico Rules of Civil Procedure,4 which govern the New Mexico Public Regulation

          Commission’s Discovery rules except in cases where the Commission’s rules are in conflict with

          the New Mexico Rules of Civil Procedure.5

                    5.    Given the number of discrete interrogatory subparts in CLC’s 9th Set, EPE’s

          provision of full responses by September 15, 2026 is reasonable, in light of CLC’s request that the

          responses be provided in full by September 16, 2026.

                    6.    Given the agreement reached between CLC and EPE for EPE’s service of

          responses, CLC’s request that EPE be ordered to respond to CLC’s Motion to Compel6 should be

          denied.

                    7.    CLC’s request to adjust testimony deadlines should not be granted at this time,

          given the agreement reached between CLC and EPE regarding CLC’s 9th Set, and the apparent

          progress being made with discovery matters in this docket.

                    It is therefore ORDERED:

                    A.    CLC’s request that EPE be required to file an expedited response to CLC’s Motion to

          Compel by September 14, 2026, is Denied.

                    B.    CLC may amend its Motion to Compel, if deemed necessary, after September 15th.

                    C.    If CLC does not file an amendment to CLC’s Motion to Compel, CLC’s Motion to Compel

          will be Moot.




